### What is the principle of least privilege?

The principle of least privilege states that every user, service, process, and credential should be granted only the minimum permissions required to perform its function, and nothing more. It is the single most effective control for limiting the blast radius of any security incident.

If a reporting service only needs to read balances, it should only have read access. If it is compromised, it cannot move money because it never had write access.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
public class LeastPrivilegeConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
            // Reporting service: read-only scope
            .requestMatchers("/api/v1/reports/**")
                .hasAuthority("SCOPE_account:read")
            // Transfer service: write scope required
            .requestMatchers("/api/v1/transfers/**")
                .hasAuthority("SCOPE_transfer:write")
            // Admin endpoints: admin scope only
            .requestMatchers("/api/v1/admin/**")
                .hasAuthority("SCOPE_admin")
            .anyRequest().denyByDefault()
        );
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

```text
Reporting service (scope=account:read):
  GET /api/v1/reports/balances --> 200 OK
  POST /api/v1/transfers      --> 403 Forbidden (no write scope)
  GET /api/v1/admin/users      --> 403 Forbidden (no admin scope)
```

### How does it apply at the database layer?

The application database user should have only the SQL statements it needs. Never grant superuser, DROP, or DELETE * to the application account.

```sql
-- Least privilege database grants
CREATE USER reporting_user WITH PASSWORD 'strong_pass';
GRANT SELECT ON accounts, transactions TO reporting_user;
-- Cannot INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, or DROP anything

CREATE USER transfer_user WITH PASSWORD 'strong_pass';
GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON transactions TO transfer_user;
GRANT SELECT, UPDATE ON accounts TO transfer_user;
-- Can read and modify needed tables, but cannot DROP or access admin tables

CREATE USER admin_user WITH PASSWORD 'strong_pass';
GRANT ALL ON ALL TABLES TO admin_user;
-- Full access, but this account is never used by the application
-- Only used by DBAs with MFA and audit logging
```

### How does it apply at the cloud IAM layer?

Cloud IAM policies should grant specific actions on specific resources rather than wildcard permissions. Roles should be assumed temporarily rather than using long-lived root credentials.

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::bank-statements-bucket/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```text
WRONG (wildcard):
  "Action": "*",
  "Resource": "*"
  --> Service can do ANYTHING to ANY resource!

CORRECT (least privilege):
  "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::bank-statements-bucket/*"
  --> Service can only read/write to one specific bucket
```

### What is just-in-time elevation?

Just-in-time (JIT) elevation grants extra permissions only for the duration needed, then automatically revokes them. An admin does not hold permanent elevated access; they request it, perform the action, and it expires.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.time.Duration;

public class JitElevationService {

    public ElevationGrant requestElevation(String adminUser, String permission,
                                           Duration duration) {
        Instant expiry = Instant.now().plus(duration);
        ElevationGrant grant = new ElevationGrant(adminUser, permission, expiry);
        System.out.println("Granted " + permission + " to " + adminUser
            + " until " + expiry);
        return grant;
    }

    public boolean isElevated(ElevationGrant grant) {
        boolean active = Instant.now().isBefore(grant.expiry());
        if (!active) {
            System.out.println("Elevation expired for " + grant.user());
        }
        return active;
    }

    record ElevationGrant(String user, String permission, Instant expiry) {}
}
```

```text
Granted account:unlock to priya_admin until 2024-10-27T10:10:00Z
10 minutes later:
Elevation expired for priya_admin
--> Admin loses elevated access automatically
```

### What is separation of duties?

Separation of duties ensures no single identity can both initiate and approve a sensitive action. In banking, one person creates a transfer and a different person approves it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.security.access.prepost.PreAuthorize;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('TELLER')")
    public String initiateTransfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount,
                                   String initiator) {
        // Teller creates the transfer request
        return "Transfer initiated by " + initiator + ": "
            + amount + " from " + from + " to " + to;
    }

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('MANAGER') and #approver != #initiator")
    public String approveTransfer(String transferId, String approver,
                                  String initiator) {
        // Manager approves, but cannot be the same person who initiated
        return "Transfer " + transferId + " approved by " + approver;
    }
}
```

```text
Priya (TELLER) initiates transfer --> OK
Priya (TELLER) approves same transfer --> 403 Forbidden (same person)
Rajesh (MANAGER) approves --> OK (different person)
```

### What are common anti-patterns?

The classic anti-patterns are wildcard permissions, shared admin accounts, and temporary grants that never get removed (privilege creep).

```text
Anti-patterns:
  1. Wildcard:  "Action": "*", "Resource": "*" (grants everything)
  2. Shared:    One admin account used by 5 people (no accountability)
  3. Creep:     Temporary admin grant given 6 months ago, never revoked
  4. Root:      Application running as root/superuser (full OS access)
  5. All-ports: Security group allowing 0.0.0.0:0-65535 (all traffic)

Fixes:
  1. Specific actions on specific resources
  2. Individual accounts with individual audit trails
  3. Regular access reviews + automated expiry
  4. Non-root service accounts with minimal permissions
  5. Allow only required ports from required sources
```

### How do you enforce least privilege in practice?

Regular access reviews and automated detection of over-privileged identities keep privilege creep in check. Tools like AWS Access Analyzer or custom scripts compare granted permissions against actually-used permissions.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import java.util.Set;

public class AccessReviewer {

    public void review(String service, Set<String> granted, Set<String> used) {
        Set<String> unused = new java.util.HashSet<>(granted);
        unused.removeAll(used);

        if (!unused.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("WARNING: " + service + " has unused permissions: "
                + unused);
            System.out.println("Recommendation: remove " + unused);
        } else {
            System.out.println(service + ": all permissions actively used.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
WARNING: reporting-service has unused permissions: [account:write, admin]
Recommendation: remove [account:write, admin]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Senior engineers know that least privilege is one of the cheapest, highest-impact controls for shrinking the damage any single compromise can cause. In banking it is frequently a regulatory requirement (PCI-DSS, SOC2), not just good hygiene. They combine it with JIT elevation, separation of duties, automated access reviews, and deny-by-default policies.
